1
00:00:00,830 --> 00:00:05,480
Chapter 2 I resisted all the way.

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:11,379
A new thing for me, and a circumstance which greatly strengthened the bad opinion

3
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:14,240
Bessie and Mrs. Abbott were disposed to entertain of me.

4
00:00:15,279 --> 00:00:18,791
The fact is, I was a trifle beside myself, or

5
00:00:18,792 --> 00:00:22,640
rather out of myself, as the French would say.

6
00:00:23,264 --> 00:00:28,860
I was conscious that a moment's mutiny had already rendered me liable to strange

7
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,794
penalties, and like any other rebel slave, I felt

8
00:00:32,796 --> 00:00:36,380
resolved in my desperation to go all lengths.

9
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:39,059
Hold her arms, Mrs. Abbott.

10
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,659
She's like a mad cat.

11
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:44,720
For shame, for shame, cried the ladies made.

12
00:00:45,379 --> 00:00:49,419
What shocking conduct, Mrs. Air, to strike a young gentleman, your

13
00:00:49,421 --> 00:00:53,659
benefactresses son, your young master, master.

14
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,500
How is he my master?

15
00:00:56,059 --> 00:00:57,059
Am I a servant?

16
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,480
No.

17
00:00:58,519 --> 00:01:01,379
You are less than a servant for you do nothing for your keep.

18
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:05,600
There, sit down and think over your wickedness.

19
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:11,307
They had got me by this time into the apartment indicated by Mrs.

20
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:14,220
Reed, and had thrust me upon a stool.

21
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:17,572
My impulse was to rise from it like a spring,

22
00:01:17,572 --> 00:01:20,600
their two pair of hands arrested me instantly.

23
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:26,019
If you don't sit still, you must be tied down, said Bessie.

24
00:01:27,139 --> 00:01:30,820
Mrs. Abbott, lend me your garters, she would break mine directly.

25
00:01:32,779 --> 00:01:36,820
Mrs. Abbott turned to divest a stout leg of the necessary ligature.

26
00:01:37,690 --> 00:01:42,039
This preparation for bonds, and the additional ignominy it inferred,

27
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:44,360
took a little of the excitement out of me.

28
00:01:45,059 --> 00:01:46,740
Don't take them off, I cried.

29
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:48,620
I will not stir.

30
00:01:49,779 --> 00:01:54,159
In guarantee we're off, I attached myself to my seat by my hands.

31
00:01:55,419 --> 00:01:59,980
Mind you don't, said Bessie, and when she had ascertained that I was really

32
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:06,539
subsiding, she loosened her hold of me, then she and Mrs. Abbott stood with folded

33
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:12,899
arms, looking darkly and doubtfully on my face as incredulous of my sanity.

34
00:02:14,434 --> 00:02:18,740
She never did so before, at last said Bessie, turning to the Abigail,

35
00:02:19,769 --> 00:02:22,200
but it was always in her, was the reply.

36
00:02:23,669 --> 00:02:28,539
I've told Mrs. often my opinion about the child, and Mrs. agreed with me.

37
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,879
She's an underhand little thing.

38
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:34,880
I never saw a girl of her age with so much cover.

39
00:02:36,069 --> 00:02:42,019
Bessie answered not, but air-long addressing me, she said, you ought to be

40
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:45,680
aware, Miss, that you are under obligations to Mrs. Reed.

41
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:47,559
She keeps you.

42
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:51,619
If she were to turn you off, you would have to go to the poor house.

43
00:02:53,559 --> 00:02:55,440
I had nothing to say to these words.

44
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,400
They were not new to me.

45
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:03,660
My very first recollections of existence included hints of the same kind.

46
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:09,584
This reproach of my dependence had become a vague sing-song in

47
00:03:09,585 --> 00:03:13,440
my ear, very painful and crushing, but only half intelligible.

48
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:15,980
Miss Abbott joined in.

49
00:03:17,309 --> 00:03:22,220
And you ought not to think yourself on an equality with the Mrs. Reed and Master

50
00:03:22,221 --> 00:03:27,359
Reed, because Mrs. kindly allows you to be brought up with them.

51
00:03:28,769 --> 00:03:32,500
They will have a great deal of money, and you will have none.

52
00:03:33,559 --> 00:03:39,101
It is your place to be humble, and to try to make yourself agreeable to them.

53
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:45,519
What we tell you is for your good, added Bessie, in no harsh voice.

54
00:03:46,509 --> 00:03:48,960
You should try to be useful and pleasant then.

55
00:03:49,559 --> 00:03:54,146
Perhaps you would have a home here, but if you become passionate and rude, Mrs.

56
00:03:54,686 --> 00:03:56,420
will send you away, I am sure.

57
00:03:57,869 --> 00:04:01,940
Besides, said Miss Abbott, God will punish her.

58
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,680
He might strike her dead in the midst of her tantrums.

59
00:04:06,285 --> 00:04:08,259
And then, where would she go?

60
00:04:09,259 --> 00:04:11,140
Come, Bessie, we will leave her.

61
00:04:11,779 --> 00:04:13,359
I wouldn't have her heart for anything.

62
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:16,740
Say your prayers, Miss Aya, when you are by yourself.

63
00:04:17,509 --> 00:04:20,036
For if you don't repent, something bad might be

64
00:04:20,037 --> 00:04:22,559
permitted to come down the chimney and fetch you away.

65
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:28,680
They went, shutting the door, and locking it behind them.

66
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:34,400
The red room was a square chamber, very seldom slept in.

67
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:36,700
I might say never, indeed.

68
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:42,900
Unless when a chance influx of visitors at Gateshead Hall rendered it necessary to

69
00:04:42,901 --> 00:04:45,859
turn to account all the accommodation it contained.

70
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,779
Yet it was one of the largest and stateless chambers in the mansion.

71
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:57,259
A bed supported on massive pillars of Mojogne, hung with curtains of deep red

72
00:04:57,384 --> 00:05:01,200
damask, stood out like a tabernacle in the centre.

73
00:05:02,194 --> 00:05:06,554
The two large windows, with their blinds always drawn down,

74
00:05:06,555 --> 00:05:10,480
were half shrouded in festoons and falls of similar drapery.

75
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:12,640
The carpet was red.

76
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:16,940
The table at the foot of the bed was covered with a crimson cloth.

77
00:05:18,269 --> 00:05:22,319
The walls were a soft fawn colour, with a blush of pink in it.

78
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:28,461
The wardrobe, the toilet table, the chairs were of darkly polished old Mojogne.

79
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:33,839
Out of these deep surrounding shades rose high and glared white.

80
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:40,500
The piled-up mattresses and pillows of the bed, spread with a snowy massay counterpan.

81
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:47,400
Scarcely less prominent was an ample cushioned easy chair near the head of the

82
00:05:47,401 --> 00:05:53,319
bed, also white, with a footstool before it, and looking, as I thought,

83
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:55,539
like a pale throne.

84
00:05:57,190 --> 00:06:00,759
This room was chill, because its seldom had a fire.

85
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:06,497
It was silent, because remote from the nursery and kitchen,

86
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:09,839
solemn, because it was known to be so seldom entered.

87
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:14,568
The house made alone came here on Saturdays, to wipe

88
00:06:14,569 --> 00:06:18,420
from the mirrors in the furniture a week's quiet dust.

89
00:06:19,519 --> 00:06:25,579
A Mrs. Reed herself, at far intervals, visited it to review the contents of a

90
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:30,160
certain secret drawer in the wardrobe, where were stored divers' parchedments,

91
00:06:30,819 --> 00:06:34,480
her jewel casket, and a miniature of her deceased husband.

92
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:40,153
And in those last words lies the secret of the red room,

93
00:06:40,153 --> 00:06:43,880
the spell which kept it so lonely in spite of its grandeur.

94
00:06:45,339 --> 00:06:47,480
Mr. Reed had been dead nine years.

95
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:53,036
It was in this chamber he breathed his last, here he lay in

96
00:06:53,037 --> 00:06:56,180
state, hence his coffin was born by the undertaker's men.

97
00:06:57,039 --> 00:06:59,895
And since that day, a sense of dreary consecration

98
00:06:59,896 --> 00:07:02,701
had guarded it from frequent intrusion.

99
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:08,500
My seat, to which Bessie and the bitter mishabbet had left me riveted,

100
00:07:09,390 --> 00:07:12,200
was a low ottoman near the marble chimney -piece.

101
00:07:13,359 --> 00:07:15,119
The bed rose before me.

102
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:19,769
To my right hand there was the high dark wardrobe, with

103
00:07:19,769 --> 00:07:23,720
subdued broken reflections varying the glass of its panels.

104
00:07:25,079 --> 00:07:29,900
To my left were the muffled windows, a great looking glass between them

105
00:07:29,901 --> 00:07:32,640
repeated the vacant majesty of the bed and room.

106
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:37,252
I was not quite sure whether they locked the door

107
00:07:37,253 --> 00:07:40,240
and when I dared move I got up and went to see.

108
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:45,839
Alas, yes, no jail was ever more secure.

109
00:07:47,039 --> 00:07:50,099
Returning, I had to cross before the looking glass.

110
00:07:51,039 --> 00:07:54,880
My fascinated glance involuntarily explored the depth it revealed.

111
00:07:55,819 --> 00:08:00,059
All looked colder and darker in that visionary hollow than in reality.

112
00:08:00,890 --> 00:08:06,660
And the strange little figure there gazing at me, with a white face and arms specking

113
00:08:06,661 --> 00:08:11,448
the gloom and glittering eyes of fear moving where

114
00:08:11,449 --> 00:08:15,559
all else was still had the effect of a real spirit.

115
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:20,700
I thought it like one of the tiny phantoms, half fairy, half imp.

116
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:27,440
Bessie's evening stories represented as coming out of lone ferny dels in moors and

117
00:08:27,441 --> 00:08:30,740
appearing before the eyes of belated travellers.

118
00:08:31,970 --> 00:08:33,220
I returned to my stool.

119
00:08:34,779 --> 00:08:37,292
Superstition was with me at that moment, but

120
00:08:37,294 --> 00:08:40,701
it was not yet her hour for complete victory.

121
00:08:41,170 --> 00:08:42,640
My blood was still warm.

122
00:08:43,559 --> 00:08:47,859
The mood of the revolted slave was still bracing me with its bitter vigor.

123
00:08:48,579 --> 00:08:52,046
I had to stem a rapid rush of retrospective

124
00:08:52,047 --> 00:08:55,000
thought before I quailed to the dismal present.

125
00:08:56,089 --> 00:09:00,299
All John Reed's violent tyrannies, all his sisters proud in difference,

126
00:09:00,539 --> 00:09:05,359
all his mothers aversion, all the servants' partiality, turned up in my

127
00:09:05,361 --> 00:09:08,779
disturbed mind like a dark deposit in a turbid well.

128
00:09:10,289 --> 00:09:11,900
Why was I always suffering?

129
00:09:12,599 --> 00:09:15,940
Always brow-beaten, always accused, forever condemned?

130
00:09:17,009 --> 00:09:18,140
Why could I never please?

131
00:09:19,150 --> 00:09:21,839
Why was it useless to try to win anyone's favour?

132
00:09:23,079 --> 00:09:26,640
Eliza who was headstrong and selfish was respected.

133
00:09:27,519 --> 00:09:33,339
Georgiana, who had a spoiled temper, a very accurate spite, a capsious and

134
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:36,559
insolent carriage, was universally indulged.

135
00:09:37,430 --> 00:09:42,980
Her beauty, her pink cheeks and golden curls seemed to give delight to all who

136
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:46,819
looked at her and to purchase indemnity for every fault.

137
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:51,200
John no-one thwarted, much less punished.

138
00:09:52,029 --> 00:09:57,220
Though he twisted the next of the pigeons, killed the little peachyx, set the dogs at

139
00:09:57,221 --> 00:10:01,232
the sheep, stripped the hot-house vines of their fruit, and

140
00:10:01,233 --> 00:10:04,460
broke the buds off the choicest plants in the conservatory.

141
00:10:05,269 --> 00:10:09,539
He called his mother old girl, too, sometimes reviled her for her dark

142
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:15,339
skin, similar to his own, bluntly disregarded her wishes, not unfrequently

143
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:20,440
tore and spoiled her silk attire, and he was still her own darling.

144
00:10:22,329 --> 00:10:23,700
I dared commit no fault.

145
00:10:24,539 --> 00:10:29,420
I strove to fulfill every duty, and I was termed naughty and tiresome,

146
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,519
sullen and sneaking, from morning to noon, and from noon to night.

147
00:10:35,059 --> 00:10:39,180
My head still ached and bled with the blow and fall I had received.

148
00:10:40,210 --> 00:10:45,160
No-one had approved John for wantonly striking me, and because I had turned

149
00:10:45,161 --> 00:10:48,006
against him to avert farther irrational

150
00:10:48,007 --> 00:10:51,721
violence, I was loaded with general oprobrium.

151
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:59,539
Unjust, unjust, said my reason, forced by the agonizing stimulus into

152
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:04,500
precocious, though transitory power, and resolve, equally brought up,

153
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:08,839
instigated some strange expedient to achieve escape from insupportable

154
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:14,278
oppression, as running away, or if that could not be affected,

155
00:11:14,278 --> 00:11:18,000
never eating or drinking more, and letting myself die.

156
00:11:19,539 --> 00:11:25,019
What a consternation of soul was mine that dreary afternoon, how all my brain was

157
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:30,500
entomaled, and all my heart in insurrection, yet in what darkness,

158
00:11:31,279 --> 00:11:34,880
what dense ignorance was the mental battle fought.

159
00:11:35,894 --> 00:11:38,710
I could not answer the ceaseless inward question.

160
00:11:39,430 --> 00:11:45,190
Why, I thus suffered, now at the distance of, I will not say how many years,

161
00:11:45,650 --> 00:11:46,650
I see it clearly.

162
00:11:48,150 --> 00:11:50,549
I was a discord in Gateshead Hall.

163
00:11:51,730 --> 00:11:53,309
I was like nobody there.

164
00:11:54,470 --> 00:12:00,730
I had nothing in harmony with Mrs. Reed or her children, or her chosen vassalage.

165
00:12:01,805 --> 00:12:06,170
If they did not love me, in fact, as little did I love them.

166
00:12:07,170 --> 00:12:09,512
They were not bound to regard with affection a thing

167
00:12:09,513 --> 00:12:11,910
that could not sympathize with one amongst them.

168
00:12:13,009 --> 00:12:20,069
A heterogeneous thing opposed to them in temperament, in capacity, in propensities,

169
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:26,609
a useless thing, incapable of serving their interest, or adding to their

170
00:12:26,611 --> 00:12:32,410
pleasure, a noxious thing, cherishing the germs of indignation at their treatment,

171
00:12:33,109 --> 00:12:34,609
of contempt of their judgment.

172
00:12:36,329 --> 00:12:42,029
I know that had I been a sanguine, brilliant, careless, exacting,

173
00:12:42,049 --> 00:12:47,951
handsome, romping child, though equally dependent and friendless, Mrs.

174
00:12:47,952 --> 00:12:51,650
Reed would have endured my presence more complacently.

175
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:57,470
Her children would have entertained for me more of the cordiality of fellow-feeling.

176
00:12:58,234 --> 00:13:02,470
The servants would have been less prone to make me the scapegoat of the nursery.

177
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:06,349
Daylight began to forsake the red room.

178
00:13:06,990 --> 00:13:13,111
It was past four o'clock, and the beclouded afternoon was tending to drier twilight.

179
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:17,809
I heard the rain still beating continuously on the staircase window,

180
00:13:18,505 --> 00:13:21,150
and the wind howling in the grove behind the hall.

181
00:13:22,289 --> 00:13:27,109
I grew by degrees cold as a stone, and then my courage sank.

182
00:13:28,190 --> 00:13:32,150
My habitual mood of humiliation, self-doubt, for lawn depression,

183
00:13:32,890 --> 00:13:35,329
fell damp on the embers of my decaying eye.

184
00:13:36,289 --> 00:13:39,470
All said I was wicked, and perhaps I might be so.

185
00:13:40,710 --> 00:13:45,349
What thought had I been but just conceiving of starving myself to death?

186
00:13:46,369 --> 00:13:49,710
That certainly was a crime, and was I fit to die?

187
00:13:50,930 --> 00:13:54,549
Or was the vault under the Chancellor of Gateshead Church an inviting born?

188
00:13:56,119 --> 00:13:59,460
In such vault, I had been told did Mr. Reed lie

189
00:13:59,461 --> 00:14:02,730
buried and led by this thought to recall his idea.

190
00:14:03,529 --> 00:14:06,210
I dwelt on it, with gathering dread.

191
00:14:07,470 --> 00:14:13,490
I could not remember him, but I knew that he was my own uncle, my mother's brother,

192
00:14:14,150 --> 00:14:19,809
that he had taken me when a parentless infant to his house, and that in his last

193
00:14:19,810 --> 00:14:25,089
moments he had required a promise of Mrs. Reed that she would rear and maintain me

194
00:14:25,515 --> 00:14:27,069
as one of her own children.

195
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:32,009
Mrs. Reed probably considered she had kept this promise.

196
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:37,710
And so she had, I dare say, as well as her nature would permit her.

197
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:43,950
But how could she really liken into Loppa not of her race and unconnected with her

198
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:47,410
after her husband's death by any tie?

199
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:53,250
It must have been most irksome to find herself bound by a hard rung pledge to

200
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:57,410
stand in the stead of a parent, to a strange child she could not love,

201
00:14:58,009 --> 00:15:03,471
and to see an uncongenial alien permanently intruded on her own family group.

202
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:06,809
A singular notion dawned upon me.

203
00:15:07,529 --> 00:15:10,269
I doubted not, never doubted.

204
00:15:10,930 --> 00:15:14,490
The Diff Mr. Reed had been alive, he would have treated me kindly,

205
00:15:15,029 --> 00:15:20,950
and now, as I sat looking at the white bed and overshadowed walls, occasionally also

206
00:15:20,951 --> 00:15:24,410
turning a fascinated eye towards the dimly gleaming mirror.

207
00:15:25,819 --> 00:15:31,029
I began to recall what I had heard of dead men, troubled in their graves by the

208
00:15:31,030 --> 00:15:34,940
violation of their last wishes, revisiting the earth

209
00:15:34,941 --> 00:15:37,950
to punish the perjured and avenge the oppressed.

210
00:15:38,940 --> 00:15:43,369
And I thought Mr. Reed's spirit, harassed by the wrongs of his sister's

211
00:15:43,371 --> 00:15:48,549
child, might quit its abode, whether in the church vault, or in the unknown world

212
00:15:48,550 --> 00:15:52,329
of the departed, and rise before me in this chamber.

213
00:15:53,900 --> 00:16:00,269
I wiped my tears and hushed my sobs, fearful, lest any sign of violent grief

214
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:05,150
might wake in a preternatural voice to comfort me, or elicit from the gloom,

215
00:16:05,329 --> 00:16:09,009
some hallowed face, bending over me with strange pity.

216
00:16:10,109 --> 00:16:15,390
This idea, consultory in theory, I felt would be terrible if realized.

217
00:16:16,450 --> 00:16:21,150
With all my might, I endeavored to stifle it, I endeavored to be firm.

218
00:16:22,390 --> 00:16:24,919
Shaking my hair from my eyes, I lifted my head

219
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:28,111
and tried to look boldly around the dark room.

220
00:16:28,819 --> 00:16:31,890
At this moment, a light gleamed on the wall.

221
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:39,490
Was it, I asked myself, array from the moon, penetrating some aperture in the blind?

222
00:16:39,569 --> 00:16:40,569
No.

223
00:16:40,710 --> 00:16:44,888
Moonlight was still, and this stirred, while I gazed,

224
00:16:44,890 --> 00:16:48,109
it glided up to the ceiling and quivered over my head.

225
00:16:49,220 --> 00:16:54,430
I can now conjecture readily that this streak of light was, in all likelihood,

226
00:16:55,269 --> 00:16:58,269
a gleam from a lantern carried by someone across the lawn.

227
00:16:59,785 --> 00:17:06,269
But then, prepared as my mind was for horror, shaken as my nerves were by

228
00:17:06,270 --> 00:17:10,387
agitation, I thought the swift, darting beam was

229
00:17:10,388 --> 00:17:13,529
a herald of some coming vision from another world.

230
00:17:14,799 --> 00:17:18,855
My heart beat thick, my head grew hot, a sound

231
00:17:18,856 --> 00:17:22,670
filled my ears, which I deemed the rushing of wings.

232
00:17:23,529 --> 00:17:29,250
Something seemed near me, I was oppressed, suffocated, endurance broke down,

233
00:17:29,990 --> 00:17:32,849
I rushed to the door and shook the lock in desperate effort.

234
00:17:33,809 --> 00:17:39,790
Steps came running along the outer passage, the key turned, Bessie and Abbott entered.

235
00:17:40,980 --> 00:17:43,730
Miss Aya, are you ill, said Bessie.

236
00:17:44,890 --> 00:17:49,430
What a dreadful noise, it went quite through me, exclaimed Abbott.

237
00:17:50,170 --> 00:17:53,430
Take me out, let me go into the nursery, was my cry.

238
00:17:55,069 --> 00:17:56,190
What for, are you hurt?

239
00:17:56,589 --> 00:17:57,589
Have you seen something?

240
00:17:57,950 --> 00:17:59,369
Again, demanded Bessie.

241
00:18:00,349 --> 00:18:03,329
Oh, I saw a light, and I thought a ghost would come.

242
00:18:04,779 --> 00:18:09,609
I had now got hold of Bessie's hand, and she did not snatch it from me.

243
00:18:11,079 --> 00:18:16,589
She has screamed out on purpose, declared Abbott, in some disgust.

244
00:18:17,470 --> 00:18:22,329
And what a scream, if she had been in great pain, one would have excused it,

245
00:18:22,954 --> 00:18:24,910
but she only wanted to bring us all here.

246
00:18:25,750 --> 00:18:27,250
I know her naughty tricks.

247
00:18:28,319 --> 00:18:34,390
What is all this, demanded another voice peremptually, and Mrs. Reed came along the

248
00:18:34,391 --> 00:18:38,349
corridor, her cap flying wide, her gown rustling stormily.

249
00:18:39,849 --> 00:18:43,376
Abbott and Bessie, I believe, I gave orders that Jane Aya

250
00:18:43,377 --> 00:18:46,670
should be left in the red room till I came to her myself.

251
00:18:48,349 --> 00:18:52,450
Miss Jane screamed so loud, ma'am, pleaded Bessie.

252
00:18:53,369 --> 00:18:55,430
Let her go, was the only answer.

253
00:18:56,390 --> 00:18:59,146
Lose Bessie's hand child, you cannot succeed

254
00:18:59,147 --> 00:19:02,191
in getting out by these means, be assured.

255
00:19:02,849 --> 00:19:06,049
I abhor artifice, particularly in children.

256
00:19:06,950 --> 00:19:09,849
It is my duty to show you that tricks will not answer.

257
00:19:10,769 --> 00:19:15,670
You will now stay here an hour longer, and it is only on condition of perfect

258
00:19:15,671 --> 00:19:19,529
submission and stillness that I shall liberate you then.

259
00:19:21,115 --> 00:19:23,109
Oh, aren't, have pity?

260
00:19:24,009 --> 00:19:25,009
Forgive me?

261
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:26,309
I cannot endure it.

262
00:19:27,089 --> 00:19:28,509
Let me be punished some other way.

263
00:19:28,950 --> 00:19:30,049
I shall be killed if...

264
00:19:30,990 --> 00:19:31,990
Silence.

265
00:19:32,650 --> 00:19:34,930
This violence is all most repulsive.

266
00:19:35,809 --> 00:19:38,409
And so, no doubt, she felt it.

267
00:19:39,389 --> 00:19:41,730
I was a precocious actress in her eyes.

268
00:19:42,329 --> 00:19:46,610
She sincerely looked on me as a compound of virulent passions, mean spirit,

269
00:19:47,230 --> 00:19:48,829
and dangerous duplicity.

270
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:56,789
Bessie and Abbott, having retreated, Mrs. Reed, impatient of my now frantic

271
00:19:56,791 --> 00:19:59,802
anguish and wild sobs, abruptly thrust me

272
00:19:59,802 --> 00:20:03,771
back and locked me in without father Parley.

273
00:20:04,659 --> 00:20:08,028
I heard her sweeping away, and soon after she

274
00:20:08,030 --> 00:20:12,371
was gone, I suppose I had a species of fit.

275
00:20:13,289 --> 00:20:15,070
Unconsciousness closed the scene.

276
00:20:17,849 --> 00:20:18,000
Chapter 3 The next thing I remember is, waking up with a feeling as if I had had a
